  "slug": "phylogenetic-tree-be52",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a publication extend tree inference to rooting sensitivity, joint sequence-phylogeny modelling, and population-size point processes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the phylogenetic tree as the object of inference when gene histories, sequence states, or population lineages must be modelled jointly or compared. The triplet-based study evaluates how gene tree rooting, or its absence, affects species tree estimation with STELAR, benchmarked against ASTRAL on simulated and empirical data. PhyloPotts couples a Potts model of sequence dependence with phylogeny through a first-order approximation, reporting improved ancestral sequence reconstruction accuracy and an open-source BEAST 2 implementation. The bounded coalescent preprint instead treats effective population size trajectories as a point process, offering exact simulation and MCMC inference, and addresses population dynamics rather than tree comparison directly. What is new is explicit treatment of rooting sensitivity in triplet-based inference and the coupling of sequence-level Potts structure with phylogeny.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-09-29"
}
